WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:19.020
THE SENTIMENT AROUND BING:
What’s the first words that come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:26.640
to mind when you think of Bing? For most of you,&nbsp;
it’s probably: slow, old, crappy, buggy, inferior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:32.640
and unappealing. I don’t blame you as Bing is&nbsp;
associated with Microsoft which is associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:39.180
with Internet Explorer, and we all know how bad&nbsp;
Internet Explorer was. In terms of market share,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:44.160
it seems like the whole world agrees. While&nbsp;
Bing’s marketshare has been rising within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:51.960
the desktop market, it’s still struggling to crack&nbsp;
even 3% while Google stands at a towering 93%. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:58.680
this seems like an open and shut case. Google&nbsp;
dominates and Bing sucks right? Well, in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:06.600
of popularity that’s true, but the same cannot&nbsp;
be said about profitability. Bing is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:12.420
substantially more lucrative than Google in&nbsp;
terms of per user revenue. In 2022, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:18.960
Bing pulled in a total of $11.59 billion worth&nbsp;
of revenue. This means that for every percentage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:25.380
of marketshare, they pull in $3.83 billion.&nbsp;
Conversely, in 2022, Google’s search business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:32.220
pulled in a total of $162.5 billion. While their&nbsp;
overall revenue blows Bing out of the water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:37.860
their revenue per percentage of marketshare&nbsp;
comes in at less than half of Bing at $1.75&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:43.800
billion. In other words, Bing is over twice as&nbsp;
lucrative as Google from a per user standpoint.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:51.660
If Google was as lucrative as Bing, their search&nbsp;
revenue itself would jump to $355 billion. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:57.600
while Bing isn’t nearly as large as Google, it&nbsp;
does seem like they’ve been able to carve out an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.880
extremely lucrative portion of the market.&nbsp;
But, this wasn’t always the case. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.800
about ten years ago, Bing was losing money&nbsp;
hand over fist. During the worst of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:13.860
Microsoft was losing over $1 billion every single&nbsp;
quarter with Bing, and they were down a total of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:20.880
$5.5 billion within just the first two years.&nbsp;
But, after Satya Nadella took over, Bing would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:27.780
finally become profitable in late 2015. And since&nbsp;
then, it’s only gotten more and more lucrative. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.400
should also mention that Bing’s new ChatGPT&nbsp;
powered search feature has had a far better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:38.280
launch to Google’s. So, here’s how Bing went from&nbsp;
abject failure to holding it’s own against Google.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:44.700
ATTRACTING USERS:
Before Bing could worry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:49.980
about profitability, they first had to build a&nbsp;
user base. You would think that the popularity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:55.680
Microsoft and Windows would make this easy, but&nbsp;
this wasn’t exactly the case because Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:02.160
was always fighting an uphill battle with Bing.&nbsp;
Here’s the thing, Bing came out in mid 2009 which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:09.360
was during the peak of the ditch internet explorer&nbsp;
movement. In 2008, IE had nearly 70% market share,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:16.260
but this would plummet to less than 30% by the end&nbsp;
of 2013. This meant that Microsoft not only had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:22.320
convince people to stay on IE, but to also ditch&nbsp;
Google and start using Bing. As you would guess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.820
this didn’t really play out all that well&nbsp;
leading to the massive losses in Bing’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:32.640
early history. But, Microsoft wasn’t willing to&nbsp;
just give in to Google. So, they started looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.680
for ways to grow their user base which leads&nbsp;
us to their two key strategies starting with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.960
incentives. Microsoft basically decided to pay&nbsp;
users to use Bing with their rewards program.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:49.260
If you’re not familiar with Bing Rewards, it’s&nbsp;
actually quite appealing. As a new Bing user,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:54.900
you’ll be paid 5 points per search for your first&nbsp;
10 searches everyday. Once you start using Bing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:59.580
regularly and Microsoft can verify that you’re&nbsp;
not just spamming searches to earn more points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:06.180
you’ll be upgraded to level 2 which is a lot more&nbsp;
lucrative. At level 2, you can earn up to 250&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:12.300
points per day which translates to 50 incentivized&nbsp;
searches. Each point is roughly worth a tenth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:19.080
a cent, so 50 searches is worth about 25 cents.&nbsp;
Clearly, switching to Bing isn’t gonna make you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:24.480
millionaire, but it can get you a free Chipotle&nbsp;
every month which is a pretty good deal for just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.340
going about your daily business with Bing.&nbsp;
It appears that a lot of people feel this way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:35.460
given that there’s a massive reddit community with&nbsp;
over a 100,000 users who focus on just maximizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:41.820
the value of rewards points. I mean, these people&nbsp;
have it down to a science. This user for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:46.260
broke down every rewards point avenue and&nbsp;
calculated that you can earn up to $22.50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:52.680
every month using Microsoft’s various offerings.&nbsp;
Clearly, Microsoft’s been able to create a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:58.020
dedicated community, but bribing isn’t their&nbsp;
only avenue of growth. Much of Bing’s search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:03.180
volume comes from their dozens of partnerships&nbsp;
and deals. Microsoft has basically partnered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:10.560
with everyone that’s not Google. The most notable&nbsp;
of these include Yahoo, AOL, DuckDuckGo, Lycos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.480
and Ecosia. It’s not just search providers&nbsp;
that Microsoft has partnered with either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:21.420
Several years ago, they partnered with Amazon and&nbsp;
entered the ecommerce space. And more recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:27.060
they partnered with Shopify to bring merchant&nbsp;
listings to Bing. On top of all this, Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:33.360
has also integrated Bing into all of their product&nbsp;
offerings. For example, if you’re searching on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:39.000
Cortana, the Xbox, Microsoft Edge, or the Windows&nbsp;
Search Bar, you’re gonna be directed to Bing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:43.500
unless you manually change your default search&nbsp;
engine. And when you put all of this together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:49.440
you get a userbase that can only be beaten out&nbsp;
by Google, and now it was time to monetize.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:53.400
SUPERIOR DEMOGRAPHICS:&nbsp;

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:59.040
While Bing was able to grow a pretty sizeable user&nbsp;
base, one of it’s biggest shortfalls is that none&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:04.620
of it’s users are exactly choosing Bing due to&nbsp;
its search merits. As we discussed, much of Bing’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:10.380
user base comes from Microsoft’s existing products&nbsp;
and partnerships or bribery. So, how could such a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:17.100
platform become more lucrative than Google? Well,&nbsp;
the answer is superior demographics. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:22.080
just think about it, what type of person would&nbsp;
switch to Bing just to earn a couple of dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:27.300
every month? Well, it’s not the average person&nbsp;
because the average person is be too concerned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.560
with staying socially acceptable. The type&nbsp;
of person who switches to Bing is the type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:36.600
of person who’s obsessed with credit card points,&nbsp;
airline points, and hotel points. They probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:42.240
keep around their broken phone for 8 months just&nbsp;
so they can save $100 bucks on black friday. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:48.960
they’re the ones that scam car salesmen. But,&nbsp;
just because they’re so conscious of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:55.200
spending doesn’t mean they’re broke. In fact,&nbsp;
they’re usually the most well off which means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:01.020
that they’re extremely valuable ad targets. Now,&nbsp;
let’s be clear here, you’re not gonna sell these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:08.520
guys on the new iPhone or HBO Max. But, an AmEx&nbsp;
platinum with a 200,000 point sign up bonus? Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:14.280
we’re talking. These people are also the most&nbsp;
likely to entertain insurance ads, mortgage ads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:20.280
and fintech product ads. In fact, 30 to 40%&nbsp;
of all searches on Bing are related to travel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:27.720
cars, health, and finances. Meanwhile, these are&nbsp;
the top searches on Google. It’s basically just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:33.240
social media platforms, retailers, and food.&nbsp;
While Google’s searches may be more relatable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:38.940
Bing’s searches are far more lucrative when it&nbsp;
comes to adsense because the long term value of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:44.400
these products are sky high. With mortgages, for&nbsp;
example, banks can make hundreds of thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:49.320
dollars with each sale, so naturally, they pay a&nbsp;
lot more to advertise their products than Walmart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:55.740
or Starbucks. We should also note that Bing’s user&nbsp;
base tends to be older with a median age of 45.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.740 --> 00:08:01.080
And given that people tend to get wealthier&nbsp;
with age, this is once again a win for Bing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:06.780
It’s not just the financial demographics that are&nbsp;
working in Bing’s favor either. Bing also tends to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:11.760
benefit from having more desktop users. As we&nbsp;
previously touched on, much of Bing’s success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:17.220
is attributable to the desktop space where they&nbsp;
almost have 10% market share. They’re virtually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:23.700
non existent within the mobile space, but this&nbsp;
isn’t exactly a bad thing because desktop users&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:29.460
tend to be far more valuable. Desktop users&nbsp;
tend to visit far more sites and they spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:34.800
far more time on each site as well. In 2020,&nbsp;
for example, the average mobile user visited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:42.480
2.67 sites while the average desktop user visited&nbsp;
3.95 sites. Similarly the average mobile user only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:49.980
spent 160 seconds on each site while the average&nbsp;
desktop user spent 351 seconds on each site. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:54.600
naturally, Bing is going to benefit from having&nbsp;
a more lucrative demographic than Google.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:57.180
THE NEW MICROSOFT:&nbsp;

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:02.760
We’ve primarily been discussing quantitative&nbsp;
points as to how Bing is progressing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:08.460
but the reality is that much of Bing’s success&nbsp;
actually has to do with qualitative points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:14.220
and especially the new Microsoft philosophy.&nbsp;
Before Satya Nadella, the Microsoft philosophy was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:20.280
that Microsoft is the absolute best company and&nbsp;
that everyone should use everything from Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:27.060
whether that’s the Windows Phone or MSN Messenger.&nbsp;
But, Satya brought a much softer perspective to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:33.720
Microsoft. Since 2015, Microsoft’s approach has&nbsp;
been more of: how do we combine our hits with our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:39.720
competitors’ hits to create the absolute best&nbsp;
product as opposed to how do we replicate our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:46.200
competitors’ success by ourselves. In other words,&nbsp;
Microsoft has shed much of their ego and they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:52.860
much more open to collaboration. For example,&nbsp;
they launched Office on iOS. But, by far their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:57.900
most humble move of all time was their decision&nbsp;
to ditch their in house browser engine and switch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:04.260
to Google’s Chromium which has done wonders for&nbsp;
Edge. It appears that Microsoft is bringing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:09.840
same approach to Bing as well. The old Microsoft&nbsp;
would’ve probably just pushed out updated version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:15.660
of Cortana and that would’ve been their AI&nbsp;
offering. But, the new Microsoft is embracing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:23.340
ChatGPT all the way. Meanwhile, it appears that&nbsp;
Google still has Microsoft’s old mindset. They’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:29.760
want everyone to use Google everything. They want&nbsp;
you to have a google phone, a google os, a google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:36.120
search engine, a google ai, a google security&nbsp;
system, and even a google based smart home. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:41.280
this approach has worked for them so well, it’s&nbsp;
only a matter of time until someone does something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:46.920
better than Google. This already happened in&nbsp;
the social media, video conferencing space, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:53.040
self driving space. I mean, Facebook destroyed&nbsp;
Google+, Zoom and Teams are destroying Hangouts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:59.340
and Tesla is destroying Waymo. None of this has&nbsp;
really hurt Google though because these industries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:07.140
didn’t affect their main business. However, the&nbsp;
same cannot be said about ChatGPT. ChatGPT is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:13.020
direct replacement to traditional search engines&nbsp;
and it’s already doing extraordinarily well. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.020 --> 00:11:18.480
Google does have their own version of ChatGPT&nbsp;
called Google Bard which is supposed to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:25.620
out soon. And who knows, it could be way better&nbsp;
than ChatGPT, but we should note that initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:31.320
impressions aren’t giving it much credit. I mean&nbsp;
just take a look at this. Microsoft was able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:37.800
to get 1 million people on a waitlist within 48&nbsp;
hours for the new ChatGPT powered Bing. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:44.760
Google ended up losing $100 billion worth of&nbsp;
marketcap after their Bard announcement. Now, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:51.480
all we know, Bard could be a massive success, but&nbsp;
this is just the latest example of Google being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:56.760
stuck in their ways just like most companies.&nbsp;
Microsoft, however, has become surprisingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.760 --> 00:12:01.920
flexible, and I’d bet that this will turn out&nbsp;
to be far more sustainable over the long term.

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:05.760
THE FUTURE OF BING:
In the end, Bing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:15.600
came out during a time when Microsoft hate was at&nbsp;
an absolute peak. People hated IE and people hated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:22.020
Windows 8, so naturally, they didn’t want to try&nbsp;
anything that came out of Microsoft. Consequently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:28.740
Microsoft ended up losing billions with Bing&nbsp;
within the first few years. They were able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:32.820
turn this around by offering appealing&nbsp;
incentives and making key partnerships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:39.000
which attracted quite a lucrative user base.&nbsp;
But, the real turnaround for Bing didn’t come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:44.940
until Microsoft stopped forcing their products to&nbsp;
an intolerable level. For the first time, people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:52.380
actually started to use Edge and Bing out of&nbsp;
free will. And now, 1 million people are excited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.380 --> 00:13:00.480
for a ChatGPT powered Bing. I mean, just think&nbsp;
about that: they’re excited for Bing. Clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:08.100
Microsoft is turning things around in a ginormous&nbsp;
way. And who knows, in a decade, Bing may not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:13.920
be the more lucrative search engine but also&nbsp;
the larger search engine. Are you guys excited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:19.920
for the new Bing? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;
drop a like if you like to see actual competition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:24.480
in the search space. And of course, consider&nbsp;
